open_questions:
  - "What was William Webster Bates's actual birth year? His 1926 obituary gives 25 Nov 1860, but the 1860 U.S. census, enumerated 4 July 1860 — five months before that date — already records him as 'William,' age 2, in the Coker household. A contemporary census showing him already age 2 is inconsistent with a birth five months later that same year; his actual birth year was more likely 1858, with the obituary's date probably a decades-later informant's approximation rather than a documented fact. Both dates are shown pending further evidence; no primary birth record has been located."
  - "What did the initials 'L. Z.' in L. Z. \"Bun\" Bates's name stand for? A Bates family Bible entry records this child's birth (1 Nov 1904) under the name 'Albert B. Bates,' not 'L.Z.' — the 'L.Z.' form may be a nickname/initials convention layered over a birth name the family's own Bible simply records as Albert. A separate Bible entry for an 'L.J. Bates' (b. 1911) appears in what looks like a distinct generational block, possibly this child's own son rather than the child himself — unconfirmed. May also bear on an OKHS fingerprint card for an 'L.L. Bates' received 10/31/1930 for burglary."
  - "Which of the two recorded birthplaces for Beulah Beatrice Bates — Greer County or Eldorado, Oklahoma — is correct?"
  - "Can a primary source (death certificate, contemporary newspaper, or similar) be located to verify Nora Minnie Bates's 1918 influenza death independently of family letters?"
  - "William's own parentage birthplace is recorded inconsistently across census years: Georgia/Georgia (1880, 1910) vs. North Carolina/North Carolina (1920). Given that his actual parents, William E. F. Bates and Sarah Coker, are both independently documented as born in Georgia, the weight of evidence favors Georgia; the 1920 entry is treated as an outlier pending further explanation."
  - "What became of William E. F. Bates after June 1860, when the Georgia penitentiary census confirms he was still incarcerated? Family accounts offer two different and seemingly irreconcilable explanations (death in 1860; desertion to the Union Army) — a pattern of family myth-making around a difficult death that also appears on the Staggs side of the family (see Florence Staggs's page). A primary death record has not yet been located; a cousin has recently surfaced bank records suggesting William Webster Bates himself had a pattern of homesteading property, taking out loans against it, defaulting, and relocating to another county — not yet personally verified, but a possible thread for future research."
  - "Whose sister was S. Ann Cheek, in whose household 19-year-old William was living in 1880? Not yet confirmed as either a paternal or maternal aunt, though family records place Bates, Coker, and Cheek as an intermarried kinship cluster across at least two prior generations."
  - "William was still alive at the time of the burglary offense underlying Bates v. State (1930 OK CR 50) — committed 19 Apr 1926 in Louis, Harmon County, the same place of his burial. It is not yet confirmed whether the 'Bob Bates' named in that case is his son Robert Webster Bates; this is held open primarily on Robert's page pending primary-source verification."
  - "Family accounts (not yet backed by any located court record) describe William orchestrating a cover-up after his son Jesse killed a boy in a bare-knuckle betting match staged at the family home, directing his son Robert to take the blame in Jesse's place. Two family tellings of this story differ in detail. Reserved for future Journal treatment; see also Robert Webster Bates's page."
---

William Webster Bates was orphaned in early childhood. His father was imprisoned for horse theft by 1860 and, on family account, dead or estranged not long after, and his mother died by 1863. William was raised by relatives rather than by either parent. By 1880 he was living as a nephew, age 19, in the household of S. Ann Cheek in Newton County, Georgia. Whether Ann Cheek was a sister of his father or of his mother is not yet established, though a separate Bates/Coker/Cheek family vital-record page shows the three families were already closely intermarried across at least two generations before William's own birth, making either connection plausible. This mirrors the childhood of the woman he would marry: Florence Arminda "Minnie" Staggs was similarly orphaned young and raised by relatives after her own mother's presumed death. Both would go on to build the large, settled homestead family recorded in Oklahoma and Texas census records, an outcome neither of their own childhoods would have predicted.